@module dart-m07 | Advanced Dart
@summary Records, sealed classes, async programming, streams, isolates, testing and Effective Dart.

@lesson dart-m07-l01 | Records | 12
## Learning objectives
- Create positional and named records
- Return multiple values from functions
- Understand record equality and typing

## Lightweight, anonymous data
A **record** (Dart 3) groups several values into one immutable object without declaring a class:

```dart
void main() {
  final point = (3, 4);                      // positional
  final user = (name: 'Ada', age: 36);       // named
  final mixed = ('id-7', score: 98, passed: true);

  print(point.$1);       // 3
  print(user.name);      // Ada
  print(mixed.$1);       // id-7
  print(mixed.score);    // 98
}
```

- Positional fields are accessed with `$1`, `$2`, ...
- Named fields are accessed by name.
- Records are **immutable**: there are no setters.

## Record types
Record types are written in parentheses and are **structural**: any record with the same shape has the same type.

```dart
(double lat, double lng) home = (38.72, -9.14);
({String title, int minutes}) lesson = (title: 'Records', minutes: 12);
```

The names in positional type annotations (`lat`, `lng`) are documentation only.

## Returning multiple values
Before records you needed a class or a `List<dynamic>` to return several values. Now:

```dart
(int min, int max) range(List<int> values) {
  var lo = values.first, hi = values.first;
  for (final v in values) {
    if (v < lo) lo = v;
    if (v > hi) hi = v;
  }
  return (lo, hi);
}

void main() {
  final (min, max) = range([7, 2, 9, 4]); // destructuring
  print('min $min, max $max');            // min 2, max 9
}
```

## Value equality
Records compare by **value**, and `hashCode` is consistent with `==`:

```dart
void main() {
  print((1, 'a') == (1, 'a'));                 // true
  print((x: 1, y: 2) == (y: 2, x: 1));         // true – named order doesn't matter
  print({(1, 2), (1, 2)}.length);              // 1
}
```

This makes records perfect as composite map keys, for example `Map<(int, int), Tile>` for a grid.

## Records vs classes
Use a record for small, local groupings (function results, tuples, map keys). Use a class when the data has behaviour, needs documentation, is part of a public API, or is used in many places.

> TIP: Combine records with patterns in `for` loops: `for (final (index, item) in list.indexed)`.

:::check Are records mutable?
No. Record fields cannot be reassigned.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Using records for large, widely shared data structures; a named class is clearer.
- Forgetting that `$1` is the first positional field (there is no `$0`).

## Summary
- Records group values: `(1, 2)`, `(name: 'Ada')`, or a mix.
- Access with `$1`... and names; they're immutable and compare by value.
- Ideal for returning multiple values and destructuring.

## Further learning
