? scenario: Your API sometimes returns `{'items': []}` and sometimes `{'items': [{'id': ...}]}`. How do you handle both cleanly?
+ A switch with map/list patterns for the empty and non-empty shapes
- Cast with as and catch exceptions
- Convert to a string and search
- Assume items is never empty
> Patterns describe each shape and bind the values safely.

@lesson dart-m07-l03 | Sealed classes | 14
## Learning objectives
- Model closed type hierarchies with `sealed`
- Get exhaustive switches over subtypes
- Represent UI states and results safely

## A closed family of types
A `sealed` class is abstract and can only be **extended or implemented in the same library**. Because the compiler knows every subtype, switches over a sealed type are checked for exhaustiveness, just like enums, but each subtype can carry different data.

```dart
sealed class LoadState {}

class Loading extends LoadState {}

class Loaded extends LoadState {
  Loaded(this.lessons);
  final List<String> lessons;
}

class Failed extends LoadState {
  Failed(this.message);
  final String message;
}

String render(LoadState state) => switch (state) {
  Loading() => 'Spinner…',
  Loaded(:final lessons) when lessons.isEmpty => 'No lessons yet',
  Loaded(:final lessons) => '${lessons.length} lessons',
  Failed(:final message) => 'Error: $message',
};

void main() {
  print(render(Loading()));
  print(render(Loaded(['Records', 'Patterns'])));
  print(render(Failed('offline')));
}
```

If you add `class Refreshing extends LoadState {}`, every switch that doesn't handle it becomes a compile error.

## Sealed vs enum vs abstract
- **enum**: fixed set of *values*, all with the same fields.
- **sealed class**: fixed set of *types*, each with its own fields.
- **abstract class**: open set of types; no exhaustiveness.

## Modelling results and events
```dart
sealed class AuthEvent {}
class SignedIn extends AuthEvent {
  SignedIn(this.userId);
  final String userId;
}
class SignedOut extends AuthEvent {}
class SessionExpired extends AuthEvent {
  SessionExpired(this.at);
  final DateTime at;
}

void handle(AuthEvent e) {
  switch (e) {
    case SignedIn(:final userId):
      print('Welcome $userId');
    case SignedOut():
      print('Bye');
    case SessionExpired(:final at):
      print('Session expired at $at');
  }
}
```

State-management libraries like Bloc lean heavily on sealed classes for states and events.

## Rules
- A sealed class is implicitly abstract (it can't be instantiated).
- Direct subtypes must be in the same library (file, or `part` files).
- Subtypes themselves can be open, `final` or `sealed`.

> TIP: Make subclasses `final class` when you don't want anyone to extend them further. That keeps the hierarchy exactly as designed.

:::check What happens when you add a new subtype to a sealed class?
Every non-exhaustive switch over that sealed type fails to compile until it handles the new subtype.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Adding a `default`/`_` case to sealed switches, losing exhaustiveness checks.
- Trying to extend a sealed class from another file.

## Summary
- `sealed` creates a closed family of subtypes in one library.
- Switches over sealed types are exhaustive and can destructure each subtype.
- Ideal for UI states, results, events and other "one of these" data.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Class modifiers ▸ sealed
