> Each sealed subtype is its own class with its own data.

@lesson dart-m07-l04 | Class modifiers | 12
## Learning objectives
- Use `base`, `interface`, `final`, `sealed` and `mixin class`
- Control how classes can be extended or implemented across libraries
- Design stable public APIs

## Why modifiers?
By default, any class can be extended, implemented and instantiated from anywhere. That's flexible, but it makes it hard for library authors to evolve classes safely: adding a method could break someone who `implements` it. Dart 3 adds **class modifiers** to state intent:

What each modifier allows **outside** its own library:
- *(no modifier)*: construct, extend and implement
- `base`: construct and extend, but not implement
- `interface`: construct and implement, but not extend
- `final`: construct only (no extend, no implement)
- `sealed`: nothing (implicitly abstract, closed hierarchy)
- `abstract`: extend and implement, but not construct

Inside the same library, the restrictions don't apply.

```dart
// A contract other packages may implement but not inherit from
abstract interface class StorageDriver {
  Future<String?> read(String key);
  Future<void> write(String key, String value);
}

// Implementation detail: nobody outside may subclass or implement it
final class FileStorageDriver implements StorageDriver {
  @override
  Future<String?> read(String key) async => null;
  @override
  Future<void> write(String key, String value) async {}
}

// Must be extended (to keep invariants in the base constructor), never implemented
base class Entity {
  Entity(this.id) : assert(id.isNotEmpty);
  final String id;
}

void main() {
  print(FileStorageDriver() is StorageDriver); // true
}
```

## base
`base` guarantees every subtype runs the base class's constructor and inherits its implementation, because outsiders can't `implement` it (which would skip both). Subclasses of a base class must be `base`, `final` or `sealed` too.

## interface
`interface` lets outsiders implement the class but not extend it, so the library author can change private implementation details without breaking subclasses.

## final
`final class` can't be extended or implemented outside its library. Use it for classes you don't want anyone to subtype, such as value objects.

## mixin class
`mixin class` can be used both as a regular class and as a mixin. Since Dart 3, plain classes can no longer be mixed in unless declared this way.

> TIP: For app code, modifiers matter less because you own every file. For packages and large modular apps, they make the intended usage explicit and protect you from accidental breaking changes.

:::check Can code in another library implement a `base class`?
No. base classes can be extended but not implemented outside their library.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Marking classes `final` in a package and later discovering users legitimately need to mock them in tests (consider `interface` instead).
- Forgetting that subclasses of `base` classes must also be `base`, `final` or `sealed`.

## Summary
- Modifiers (`base`, `interface`, `final`, `sealed`, `abstract`, `mixin class`) control construction, extension and implementation.
- Restrictions apply across library boundaries.
- They help package authors evolve APIs safely.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Class modifiers
