@lesson dart-m07-l06 | Metadata and annotations | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use built-in annotations like `@override` and `@Deprecated`
- Understand `package:meta` annotations
- Know how code generators use annotations

## Annotations add information
**Metadata** (annotations) attaches extra information to declarations. Annotations start with `@` and are either a constant reference or a const constructor call.

```dart
class Base {
  void run() => print('base');
}

class Child extends Base {
  @override
  void run() => print('child');

  @Deprecated('Use run() instead. Will be removed in 3.0.')
  void execute() => run();
}

void main() {
  final c = Child();
  c.run();
  // c.execute(); // the analyzer shows a deprecation warning
}
```

Built-in annotations:
- `@override`: the member overrides an inherited one.
- `@Deprecated('message')` / `@deprecated`: discourages use; the analyzer warns callers.
- `@pragma('vm:prefer-inline')`: hints for compilers (advanced).

## package:meta
The `meta` package (re-exported by Flutter's `foundation`) adds annotations the analyzer understands:

```dart
import 'package:meta/meta.dart';

class Repository {
  @protected
  void cacheResult(String key) {}   // warn if used outside subclasses

  @visibleForTesting
  int get cacheSize => 0;           // warn if used outside tests

  @mustCallSuper
  void dispose() {}                 // overrides must call super.dispose()
}

@immutable
class Point {
  const Point(this.x, this.y);
  final int x;
  final int y;
}
```

`@immutable` makes the analyzer warn if a subclass adds non-final fields. Flutter's `Widget` is annotated with it.

## Annotations for code generation
Packages like `json_serializable`, `freezed` and `riverpod_generator` read annotations at build time and generate code:

```dart
// @JsonSerializable()
// class User {
//   User(this.name);
//   final String name;
//   factory User.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) => _$UserFromJson(json);
// }
```

Running `dart run build_runner build` produces the `_$UserFromJson` function in a `.g.dart` file.

## Custom annotations
Any const object can be an annotation:

```dart
class Todo {
  const Todo(this.owner, this.note);
  final String owner;
  final String note;
}

@Todo('ada', 'Add retries')
void fetchData() {}
```

Custom annotations do nothing on their own; they're read by tools (analyzer plugins or generators).

> TIP: Treat deprecation warnings as a to-do list. Run `dart fix --apply` to automatically migrate many deprecated APIs.

:::check What does @mustCallSuper do?
It makes the analyzer warn when an override of that method does not call super.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Expecting annotations to change runtime behaviour on their own.
- Ignoring `@Deprecated` warnings until an upgrade breaks the build.

## Summary
- Annotations attach metadata with `@`.
- `@override` and `@Deprecated` are built in; `package:meta` adds `@protected`, `@visibleForTesting`, `@mustCallSuper`, `@immutable`.
- Code generators read annotations to produce boilerplate.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Metadata
- pub.dev ▸ meta
